1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:03,047
October eleventh, nineteen seventy-three.

2
00:00:03,047 --> 00:00:11,124
Two men walk into a Mississippi sheriff's office and say they were taken aboard a craft by creatures with claws for hands.

3
00:00:11,124 --> 00:00:14,633
Then the deputies leave them alone, and press record.

4
00:00:15,099 --> 00:00:17,399
The sheriff wanted them to slip.

5
00:00:17,399 --> 00:00:21,064
He wanted a laugh, a whisper, a crack in the story.

6
00:00:21,064 --> 00:00:26,166
What the tape caught is one of the strangest recordings in UFO history.

7
00:00:26,633 --> 00:00:29,777
So did two shipyard workers meet real aliens?

8
00:00:29,777 --> 00:00:36,833
Or did something stranger happen inside their own heads, and the tape just caught two frightened men?

9
00:00:37,299 --> 00:00:40,738
Tonight we treat it like a recording under analysis.

10
00:00:40,738 --> 00:00:45,299
We play it, we check it against the record, and we score every claim.

11
00:00:45,766 --> 00:00:47,692
Facts first, theories labelled.

12
00:00:47,692 --> 00:00:48,500
Tape rolling.

13
00:00:52,966 --> 00:00:53,903
Chapter one.

14
00:00:53,903 --> 00:00:54,606
The pier.

15
00:00:54,606 --> 00:01:00,851
Pascagoula, Mississippi, a Gulf Coast shipbuilding town, nineteen seventy-three.

16
00:01:00,851 --> 00:01:03,271
The autumn was already strange.

17
00:01:03,271 --> 00:01:07,800
A wave of UFO reports had swept Louisiana and Mississippi.

18
00:01:08,266 --> 00:01:21,133
On the evening of the eleventh of October, Charles Hickson, forty-five, and his young relative Calvin Parker, seventeen, are fishing off an old pier on the west bank of the Pascagoula River.

19
00:01:21,599 --> 00:01:28,236
Around eight that evening, a service station operator named Larry Booth had already phoned the sheriff.

20
00:01:28,236 --> 00:01:33,133
He reported a strange craft with a blinking coloured light passing overhead.

21
00:01:33,599 --> 00:01:37,206
Then, the men say, they hear a whirring, whizzing sound.

22
00:01:37,206 --> 00:01:45,000
They turn, and see two flashing blue lights, and an oval object, thirty to forty feet across, hovering close to the bank.

23
00:01:45,466 --> 00:01:48,427
Hickson says an opening appeared in the side.

24
00:01:48,427 --> 00:01:50,072
Three creatures came out.

25
00:01:50,072 --> 00:01:52,309
They had pincers instead of hands.

26
00:01:52,309 --> 00:01:55,533
And they floated him and Parker toward the craft.

27
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:59,119
His description is odd, and oddly specific.

28
00:01:59,119 --> 00:02:00,570
Grey, wrinkled skin.

29
00:02:00,570 --> 00:02:01,876
Claws like a crab.

30
00:02:01,876 --> 00:02:05,866
Something like a slit for a mouth, and no visible eyes.

31
00:02:06,333 --> 00:02:11,646
Inside, Hickson says a mechanical eye floated over his body and examined him.

32
00:02:11,646 --> 00:02:16,199
Parker, he said, fainted at the very start and remembered nothing.

33
00:02:16,666 --> 00:02:20,508
Then the creatures released them, and the craft shot away.

34
00:02:20,508 --> 00:02:24,813
Hickson was left on the pier with a boy who was barely conscious.

35
00:02:24,813 --> 00:02:26,005
That is the story.

36
00:02:26,005 --> 00:02:26,866
Now the tape.

37
00:02:27,333 --> 00:02:28,136
Chapter two.

38
00:02:28,136 --> 00:02:29,541
The sheriff's office.

39
00:02:29,541 --> 00:02:31,949
The men do not call police at first.

40
00:02:31,949 --> 00:02:36,699
They drive to the office of the local newspaper, The Mississippi Press.

41
00:02:37,166 --> 00:02:38,212
Nobody is there.

42
00:02:38,212 --> 00:02:45,400
So, around eleven that night, they go to the Jackson County sheriff's office instead and ask to make a report.

43
00:02:45,866 --> 00:02:50,277
Captain Glenn Ryder later told reporters he suspected a prank.

44
00:02:50,277 --> 00:02:52,412
Then he looked at the two men.

45
00:02:52,412 --> 00:02:53,835
Hickson was shaking.

46
00:02:53,835 --> 00:02:58,033
Parker was in a state of what officers described as terror.

47
00:02:58,500 --> 00:03:01,535
Ryder did smell alcohol on Hickson's breath.

48
00:03:01,535 --> 00:03:05,330
Hickson said he drank whiskey after they were released.

49
00:03:05,330 --> 00:03:08,366
Ryder told the press the men were not drunk.

50
00:03:08,833 --> 00:03:10,196
Then came the trick.

51
00:03:10,196 --> 00:03:18,033
The officers left the two men alone in a room, and secretly recorded what they said, hoping to catch them in a lie.

52
00:03:18,500 --> 00:03:22,072
If it was a hoax, this was the moment it should have collapsed.

53
00:03:22,072 --> 00:03:22,866
Chapter three.

54
00:03:23,333 --> 00:03:24,319
Chapter three.

55
00:03:24,319 --> 00:03:24,953
The tape.

56
00:03:24,953 --> 00:03:28,265
Here is what the recording reportedly contains.

57
00:03:28,265 --> 00:03:30,308
Two men who sound frightened.

58
00:03:30,308 --> 00:03:32,915
Parker is heard saying he feels sick.

59
00:03:32,915 --> 00:03:35,099
Hickson tells him to calm down.

60
00:03:35,566 --> 00:03:39,200
They talk about what they saw and what they will say.

61
00:03:39,200 --> 00:03:40,503
The accounts match.

62
00:03:40,503 --> 00:03:45,166
There is no laughter, no rehearsal, no sign of a joke being enjoyed.

63
00:03:45,633 --> 00:03:50,570
Ryder said afterwards that they did everything they knew to break the stories.

64
00:03:50,570 --> 00:03:56,266
Later he told The Washington Post that if the men were lying, they should be in Hollywood.

65
00:03:56,733 --> 00:03:59,622
This is the strongest card the case holds.

66
00:03:59,622 --> 00:04:01,618
Sincere fear is hard to fake.

67
00:04:01,618 --> 00:04:08,566
But sincere fear is also what you would hear from a man who truly believes something happened to him.

68
00:04:09,033 --> 00:04:12,575
What a tape cannot do is tell us what happened.

69
00:04:12,575 --> 00:04:15,891
It proves emotion, not an object in the sky.

70
00:04:15,891 --> 00:04:17,549
Evidence of fear: yes.

71
00:04:17,549 --> 00:04:19,433
Evidence of aliens: none.

72
00:04:19,899 --> 00:04:21,312
So the tape stands.

73
00:04:21,312 --> 00:04:23,839
A real recording of real distress.

74
00:04:23,839 --> 00:04:26,366
Next, the doctors and the experts.

75
00:04:26,833 --> 00:04:27,790
Chapter four.

76
00:04:27,790 --> 00:04:28,674
The experts.

77
00:04:28,674 --> 00:04:32,135
The next day, the story hits the wire services.

78
00:04:32,135 --> 00:04:37,438
The Associated Press and United Press International carry it nationwide.

79
00:04:37,438 --> 00:04:40,900
The men are examined at Keesler Air Force Base.

80
00:04:41,366 --> 00:04:43,981
Doctors find no radiation exposure.

81
00:04:43,981 --> 00:04:45,550
The men are released.

82
00:04:45,550 --> 00:04:51,899
Local people call for federal investigators, but federal investigators do not arrive.

83
00:04:52,366 --> 00:04:54,943
Instead come two famous UFO men.

84
00:04:54,943 --> 00:04:55,990
Astronomer J.

85
00:04:55,990 --> 00:05:02,111
Allen Hynek, once the Air Force's debunker, now a believer in serious study.

86
00:05:02,111 --> 00:05:04,366
And researcher James Harder.

87
00:05:04,833 --> 00:05:08,491
They interview the two men separately, under hypnosis.

88
00:05:08,491 --> 00:05:12,421
They tell the press the accounts are likely based on fact.

89
00:05:12,421 --> 00:05:13,843
It sounds convincing.

90
00:05:13,843 --> 00:05:17,366
It is also a method we now treat with great caution.

91
00:05:17,833 --> 00:05:20,908
Hypnosis does not unlock a perfect memory.

92
00:05:20,908 --> 00:05:28,158
It can make you more confident in what you say, including details you assembled during the session.

93
00:05:28,158 --> 00:05:30,208
Hynek's endorsement is real.

94
00:05:30,208 --> 00:05:31,600
His method is weak.

95
00:05:32,066 --> 00:05:35,352
A local polygraph examiner is also brought in.

96
00:05:35,352 --> 00:05:39,566
He concludes Hickson told the truth as to what he believed.

97
00:05:39,566 --> 00:05:43,066
He adds that he is not saying a spaceship landed.

98
00:05:43,533 --> 00:05:45,030
And that is the catch.

99
00:05:45,030 --> 00:05:47,685
A polygraph measures stress, not truth.

100
00:05:47,685 --> 00:05:51,225
By the nineteen eighties it was largely discredited.

101
00:05:51,225 --> 00:05:54,833
And Parker, through his lawyer, declined to take one.

102
00:05:55,300 --> 00:05:56,181
Chapter five.

103
00:05:56,181 --> 00:05:57,129
The celebrity.

104
00:05:57,129 --> 00:06:00,383
Within weeks, Hickson is on national television.

105
00:06:00,383 --> 00:06:03,568
The Dick Cavett Show on the second of November.

106
00:06:03,568 --> 00:06:05,060
The Mike Douglas Show.

107
00:06:05,060 --> 00:06:07,500
The Tonight Show with Johnny Carson.

108
00:06:07,966 --> 00:06:12,141
Local journalists note that the men are not showing up for work.

109
00:06:12,141 --> 00:06:14,489
That fuels talk they are being paid.

110
00:06:14,489 --> 00:06:18,533
There is no proof of that, but the questions will not go away.

111
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:27,652
In January nineteen seventy-four, Hickson is arrested for public drunkenness after being found wandering along a highway.

112
00:06:27,652 --> 00:06:31,800
In April he says he is in ongoing contact with the beings.

113
00:06:32,266 --> 00:06:36,818
He tells a UFO group in Detroit that more encounters happened.

114
00:06:36,818 --> 00:06:41,884
He says a message and proof will come, to be delivered to Washington.

115
00:06:41,884 --> 00:06:43,866
No such proof ever arrives.

116
00:06:44,333 --> 00:06:50,658
In April, he even meets Erich von Daniken, the best-known ancient astronauts writer.

117
00:06:50,658 --> 00:06:56,833
The Pascagoula story becomes part of a larger myth: that visitors shaped our past.

118
00:06:57,300 --> 00:07:03,767
In nineteen eighty-three, Hickson self-publishes a book, UFO Contact at Pascagoula.

119
00:07:03,767 --> 00:07:07,352
Parker, for his part, stays quiet for decades.

120
00:07:07,352 --> 00:07:09,066
That contrast matters.

121
00:07:09,533 --> 00:07:10,438
Chapter six.

122
00:07:10,438 --> 00:07:11,418
The skeptics.

123
00:07:11,418 --> 00:07:17,677
Aviation journalist Philip Klass investigates and finds discrepancies in the story.

124
00:07:17,677 --> 00:07:22,881
He notes that Hickson refused a polygraph by an experienced examiner.

125
00:07:22,881 --> 00:07:24,766
His conclusion is a hoax.

126
00:07:25,233 --> 00:07:29,093
Investigator Joe Nickell suggests something different.

127
00:07:29,093 --> 00:07:31,023
Not a hoax, a waking dream.

128
00:07:31,023 --> 00:07:37,600
A hypnagogic state, where the mind drifts between waking and sleep and invents vivid scenes.

129
00:07:38,066 --> 00:07:42,916
Nickell points out that Hickson changed and embellished details over time.

130
00:07:42,916 --> 00:07:46,783
And that Parker's agreement could come from suggestibility.

131
00:07:46,783 --> 00:07:49,733
Parker first said he passed out at the start.

132
00:07:50,199 --> 00:07:51,684
That last point is key.

133
00:07:51,684 --> 00:07:56,652
If Parker was unconscious from the beginning, he cannot corroborate anything.

134
00:07:56,652 --> 00:08:01,233
He can only confirm that he was frightened and that something happened.

135
00:08:01,699 --> 00:08:10,004
Others have suggested ordinary explanations for the lights: a boat on the river, a helicopter, a swamp gas effect.

136
00:08:10,004 --> 00:08:12,699
None of those has been proved either.

137
00:08:13,166 --> 00:08:16,169
So the sceptics have a lot of questions.

138
00:08:16,169 --> 00:08:18,646
But they also lack a smoking gun.

139
00:08:18,646 --> 00:08:19,697
No confession.

140
00:08:19,697 --> 00:08:20,297
No fake.

141
00:08:20,297 --> 00:08:21,949
No evidence of a plan.

142
00:08:21,949 --> 00:08:23,000
Chapter seven.

143
00:08:23,466 --> 00:08:24,496
Chapter seven.

144
00:08:24,496 --> 00:08:25,525
The aftermath.

145
00:08:25,525 --> 00:08:30,747
Hickson dies in September twenty eleven of a heart attack, aged eighty.

146
00:08:30,747 --> 00:08:32,733
He never changed his story.

147
00:08:33,200 --> 00:08:36,428
Parker lived with it for the rest of his life.

148
00:08:36,428 --> 00:08:40,851
In ninety-three he started a company called UFO Investigations.

149
00:08:40,851 --> 00:08:46,466
In twenty eighteen he published his own book, Pascagoula: The Closest Encounter.

150
00:08:46,933 --> 00:08:51,592
In June twenty nineteen, a historical marker is unveiled at the site.

151
00:08:51,592 --> 00:08:52,605
Parker attends.

152
00:08:52,605 --> 00:08:57,466
He says he wished he could be buried right there, underneath the plaque.

153
00:08:57,933 --> 00:09:03,671
The city of Pascagoula now holds a festival every October to mark the anniversary.

154
00:09:03,671 --> 00:09:07,799
A frightening night has become part of the town's identity.

155
00:09:08,266 --> 00:09:13,824
Parker died of kidney cancer in August twenty twenty-three, aged sixty-eight.

156
00:09:13,824 --> 00:09:18,733
Netflix covered the case the year after in Files of the Unexplained.

157
00:09:19,200 --> 00:09:24,417
Both men told essentially the same core story for the rest of their lives.

158
00:09:24,417 --> 00:09:27,237
That consistency is a point of evidence.

159
00:09:27,237 --> 00:09:30,833
It is also exactly what a shared belief looks like.

160
00:09:31,299 --> 00:09:32,851
Time to score the case.

161
00:09:32,851 --> 00:09:33,458
Tape off.

162
00:09:33,458 --> 00:09:34,133
Lights on.

163
00:09:34,600 --> 00:09:36,200
Time to score Pascagoula.

164
00:09:36,666 --> 00:09:42,060
Two men reporting a frightening event, consistent for decades: evidence.

165
00:09:42,060 --> 00:09:44,832
The tape showing real fear: evidence.

166
00:09:44,832 --> 00:09:48,278
Officers believing they were sincere: partial.

167
00:09:48,278 --> 00:09:50,900
Hypnosis by Hynek and Harder: weak.

168
00:09:51,366 --> 00:09:53,262
The polygraph: discredited.

169
00:09:53,262 --> 00:09:55,158
A hoax for money: unproven.

170
00:09:55,158 --> 00:09:58,599
Creatures from another world: no evidence at all.

171
00:09:59,066 --> 00:09:59,926
The verdict.

172
00:09:59,926 --> 00:10:02,935
Unexplained experience, not proven aliens.

173
00:10:02,935 --> 00:10:06,230
Something frightened two men badly that night.

174
00:10:06,230 --> 00:10:09,311
But there is no craft, no trace, no object.

175
00:10:09,311 --> 00:10:13,466
The story remains a testimony, and testimony is not proof.

176
00:10:13,933 --> 00:10:17,012
And that is what makes Pascagoula so human.

177
00:10:17,012 --> 00:10:20,090
No mastermind, no money trail, no wreckage.

178
00:10:20,090 --> 00:10:26,033
Just two men, a river, and a night that neither of them ever stopped talking about.

179
00:10:26,500 --> 00:10:29,747
Next case: nineteen forty-seven, Maury Island.

180
00:10:29,747 --> 00:10:35,325
The first UFO story to be called a cover-up, and the first to be called a hoax.

181
00:10:35,325 --> 00:10:36,666
Which one was true?

182
00:10:37,133 --> 00:10:39,520
What do you think happened on that pier?

183
00:10:39,520 --> 00:10:44,533
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
